According to previous reports 13 ,  14 ,  15 – 16 , HE in martensitic steels is mostly observed due to crack propagation along (i) prior austenite grain boundaries, (ii) martensite boundaries (interfaces between martensite variants that origin from one austenite grain), (iii) {001} planes (cleavage planes for body-centered cubic (BCC) crystals), (iv) {011} planes (typical slip plane family of dislocations in BCC), and (v) interface of inclusions or precipitates as illustrated in Fig.  1 b. Consequently, interfaces including grain boundaries (GB), which largely govern materials’ properties with their structure and chemistry 17 , are critical to the HE susceptibility of the system. Ab initio calculations can probe the likelihood of hydrogen segregation at a solute-doped GB through the interaction energy between hydrogen and the doped defect ( E H-X-GB inter \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
